.TH SU 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
su \- switch to another user's identity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B su
.RI [ user ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR command ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Switch to
.IR user 's
identity (default
.BR root ),
after verifying
.IR user 's
password, then run their login shell with a clean environment. If
.BR \-c
.I command
is given, runs
.I command
through that shell instead of starting it interactively. Already
being root skips the password prompt, same as every other
.BR su .
.SH SECURITY
.B This has not been independently security-reviewed.
It only functions at all when installed setuid\-root
(owned by root,
.BR "chmod u+s" );
until then it cannot read
.B /etc/shadow
and every attempt fails, which is the safe default. Read
.B common::auth
in the source before installing this setuid anywhere that matters --
privilege\-management bugs are categorically more serious than a bug in
any other command in this suite. Get it reviewed and test it
exhaustively in a disposable VM first.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR and \fB\-\-version\fR, and \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Non-zero on a failed login, missing user, or usage error -- `su` otherwise never returns at all, since a successful run execs the target shell in place of this process.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sudo (1),
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7)
